# Normalise line endings in the repository, check out native.
#
# Without this, Git warns on every add and the working tree ends up mixed --
# some files LF, some CRLF -- which shows up as whole-file diffs the first time
# anyone on another machine touches them.
#
# `text=auto` stores everything Git detects as text with LF and hands it back
# with the platform's own endings. The toolchain here is w64devkit, whose gcc
# reads either, and the asset parsers in txt.h skip \r explicitly, so nothing
# in this project depends on which one lands on disk.
* text=auto

# The one exception. .bat is executed by cmd.exe, which needs CRLF on the
# label and goto lines to behave; checking one out with LF endings can break
# it in ways that look like a corrupt file rather than a line-ending problem.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary: never normalised, never diffed as text.
#
# NOT *.obj -- in most C projects that means a compiled object file, but here
# assets/crate.obj is a hand-commented Wavefront mesh and wants to diff as the
# text it is. Compiled objects are excluded by .gitignore instead.
*.ppm  binary
*.png  binary
